1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,810
A Japanese plane is pulled toward you.

2
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:07,390
A thin shaving curls up out of the block.

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:10,503
This time, the rest of the cutting kit:

4
00:00:10,813 --> 00:00:17,183
chisels, the hammer, and the plane,
each one beside its Western cousin.

5
00:00:23,967 --> 00:00:28,017
Japanese chisels, nomi,
come in two families.

6
00:00:28,207 --> 00:00:31,017
Tataki-nomi are struck with a hammer.

7
00:00:31,207 --> 00:00:35,037
Tsuki-nomi are pushed
by hand, with no hammer.

8
00:00:35,367 --> 00:00:40,677
A struck chisel has an iron hoop
fitted around the end of its handle.

9
00:00:41,307 --> 00:00:44,157
This hoop is called the katsura.

10
00:00:48,367 --> 00:00:53,417
The general bench chisel, struck
with a hammer, is the oire-nomi.

11
00:00:54,087 --> 00:00:59,087
You'll also see it read
oiire, or written ōire.

12
00:00:59,927 --> 00:01:05,337
For deep mortises there is the
mukōmachi-nomi, with a thick blade.

13
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:11,437
Now look at the blade itself.

14
00:01:11,967 --> 00:01:15,367
A woodworking chisel is
bevelled on one side only.

15
00:01:15,407 --> 00:01:17,977
And it's made of two layers:

16
00:01:18,267 --> 00:01:22,477
a thin layer of hard
steel, hagane, forge-welded

17
00:01:23,027 --> 00:01:26,917
to a thicker body of softer iron, jigane.

18
00:01:27,347 --> 00:01:31,127
The softer iron supports
the brittle steel and

19
00:01:31,167 --> 00:01:34,017
absorbs shock, so the steel doesn't break.

20
00:01:34,697 --> 00:01:37,477
One Japanese blade maker puts it simply:

21
00:01:38,107 --> 00:01:43,517
foreign blades are usually one piece of
steel; Japanese blades are laminated.

22
00:01:43,867 --> 00:01:48,937
The back of the blade has a
shallow hollow, the urasuki.

23
00:01:49,467 --> 00:01:52,177
It makes the back easier to flatten.

24
00:01:52,727 --> 00:01:55,257
The hollow is in the back, not the bevel:

25
00:01:55,507 --> 00:01:57,493
the chisel is not hollow-ground.

26
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,720
The hammer is the gennō.

27
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:09,710
One face is flat; the
other is slightly domed.

28
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:14,600
Driving a nail, you strike with
the flat face, then give the last

29
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:18,390
blows with the domed face,
so the surface isn't marked.

30
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:21,460
The domed face is the kigoroshi-men:

31
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:23,930
literally, the wood-killing face.

32
00:02:27,667 --> 00:02:29,617
The plane is the kanna.

33
00:02:30,267 --> 00:02:32,597
Like the saw, it's pulled toward you.

34
00:02:33,087 --> 00:02:35,277
Western and Chinese planes are pushed.

35
00:02:35,733 --> 00:02:41,183
The blade sits in a wooden
block, the dai, usually of oak.

36
00:02:41,853 --> 00:02:47,383
In the national skills-test manual, the
blade is set by tapping with a hammer:

37
00:02:48,033 --> 00:02:51,713
on the blade's head to
set it deeper, and on the

38
00:02:51,753 --> 00:02:55,283
corners of the block's
head end to draw it back.

39
00:02:57,633 --> 00:03:04,043
A two-blade plane, nimai-ganna,
adds a second iron behind the blade:

40
00:03:04,393 --> 00:03:06,583
the chip breaker, uragane.

41
00:03:07,153 --> 00:03:09,283
It helps stop tear-out.

42
00:03:09,873 --> 00:03:14,843
Like the ryōba, it is a Meiji-era
development, not an ancient one.

43
00:03:18,933 --> 00:03:22,383
If you work with Western
tools, the jobs are the same:

44
00:03:23,053 --> 00:03:27,123
bench chisels, a hammer
or mallet, a bench plane.

45
00:03:27,753 --> 00:03:30,223
The plane is the clearest difference:

46
00:03:30,673 --> 00:03:32,583
a Western plane is pushed.

47
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:38,710
Chisels and planes depend on a sharp edge.

48
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:44,180
The skills-test manual warns that
a dull tool invites injury, and

49
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:48,370
never to point a chisel's edge
at yourself or anyone else.

50
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:52,910
It also says to sharpen chisels
and planes well after use.

51
00:03:55,367 --> 00:03:57,677
Next time, our first joint:

52
00:03:58,087 --> 00:04:00,337
the koshikake ari-tsugi.

53
00:04:01,333 --> 00:04:04,343
Strike the chisel, or push it.

54
00:04:04,513 --> 00:04:07,043
Tap the blade into its block.

55
00:04:07,573 --> 00:04:09,103
Pull the plane.

56
00:04:09,593 --> 00:04:11,983
And keep every edge sharp.

57
00:04:21,867 --> 00:04:22,867
One note:

58
00:04:22,937 --> 00:04:27,827
sharp-tool technique, and how
to use these tools safely, is

59
00:04:27,867 --> 00:04:31,797
best learned from a teacher,
at a bench, in person.
